", "move": "pass", "lead_before": 3.92, "lead_after": 3.87, "points_lost": 0.04, "best": "J1"}, {"turn": 80, "color": "W", "move": "pass", "lead_before": 3.87, "lead_after": 4.0, "points_lost": 0.13, "best": "A1"}], "review": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "title": "A Nine-Point Gift at C6", "summary": "Opus 5.5 (Black) beat the 14k by 4 points under Chinese scoring with 7 komi. The middle game swung back and forth, with Black's lead moving between -2.9 and +4.3 over turns 18 to 21. The decisive moment was the 14k's C6 on turn 24, which lost 9.8 points and turned a -0.2 game into +9.6 for Black. Opus 5.5 gave back some of that at once, but the lead settled near +4 and held through the endgame until both sides passed.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "Opus 5.5's E2 lost 3.0 points, moving the lead from -0.6 to -3.6. The engine preferred D6. It was the largest early slip and left Black behind going into the fight."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 14k's B3 lost 6.8 points and flipped the game from -2.5 to +4.3 for Black. It was the first big gift, and it came right after White's earlier lead."}, {"turn": 19, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with E5, which lost 7.2 points, the worst move of its game. The lead went from +4.3 to -2.9, and the engine wanted E8. The 14k's D5 on the next turn lost 7.0 and returned the lead to +4.1."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "The 14k's C6 was the biggest error of the game at 9.8 points, taking the lead from -0.2 to +9.6 for Black. It came right after Opus 5.5 had built a row of stones on D6, E6 and F6. The engine's choice was F8."}, {"turn": 25, "text": "Opus 5.5 played F7 and lost 4.9 points, cutting a +9.6 lead to +4.7. The engine preferred D7. The cushion that remained was just enough, since the game finished at B+4."}, {"turn": 32, "text": "The 14k's D1 on the bottom edge lost 1.8 points, moving the lead from +2.4 to +4.2. Opus 5.5 answered C1 and the lead stayed at +4.2. From here the endgame was quiet, with almost every move losing 0.3 points or less."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py applies one named move to a pasted board, removes captures and prints each group's liberties; count.py counts stones and enclosed territory; libs.py prints group liberties. The player named every candidate itself (a few per call at most), and none of the scripts loops over empty points to score moves or plays continuations forward, so all of it works on the current position.", "stop": false}}